IN THE SUPREME COURT OF INDIA
CASE NO: Writ Petition (Crl.) No. 76 of 2016
CITATION: (2018) 10 SCC 1
YEAR: 2018

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Judges: Dipak Misra, R.F. Nariman, A.M. Khanwilkar, D.Y. Chandrachud, Indu Malhotra

PARTIES:
Navtej Singh Johar vs Union of India

STATUTES & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Penal Code, 1860, Constitution of India

KEY PROVISIONS & SECTIONS:
Section 377 IPC, Article 14, Article 15, Article 19, Article 21

LEGAL KEYWORDS & TOPICS:
decriminalization of section 377, consensual relations, lgbtq rights, constitutional morality

================================================================================
                    JUDGMENT SUMMARY & RATIO DECIDENDI
================================================================================

Unanimously read down Section 377 IPC to decriminalize consensual same-sex relations between adults, holding that discrimination based on sexual orientation violates constitutional guarantees.

1. ISSUES CONSIDERED:
   The court addressed questions of legal interpretation under Section 377 IPC, Article 14, Article 15, Article 19, Article 21.

2. ANALYSIS & PRECEDENT CONTEXT:
   Having examined the statutory scheme, fundamental principles of jurisprudence,
   and constitutional safeguards, the Court reiterated the necessity of strict
   compliance with statutory procedure and evidentiary standards.

3. HELD & BINDING RATIO:
   The principle formulated in this judgment constitutes binding judicial precedent
   within its territorial and appellate jurisdiction.

